        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>About</h2>
            
            <p>Originally from Italy, I come from a family of makers, artists, and business owners. As a child I dreamt first of becoming a magician, then of designing chairs. And while neither of those dreams came true, I didn’t land far off.</p>
            <p>These days, I spend my time making complex stories a little less complex, and all the while drawing audiences closer to worthwhile products, services and creative talent. </p>
            <p>
            More than its visual output, the heart of my work lies in guiding teams through major transitions, brand upgrades, and cycles of growth; producing comprehensive research and documentation; and providing clarity and structure where there was once chaos.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>Philosophy</h2>
            
            <p>We live in turbulent times. There is an overabundance of information, and in the midst of this turmoil, I see designers taking on facilitator roles and helping people communicate ideas.</p>
            <p>My bread and butter are graphic design and copywriting, the two more charming children of marketing.</p>
            <p>Speaking of marketing: I specialise in the trust-based kind, or the art of building credibility and long-term relationships with your audience by being honest, helpful and consistent. This is less of a specialty and more an approach, and it’s the one also used by successful brands like Oatly, Patagonia, Dove, WeTransfer, and plenty more.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-m" >
            <h2>General questions</h2>
            
            <details>
                <summary>What sets your approach apart?</summary>
                <p>My approach is so hands-on that I can often handle virtually every aspect of every project myself. This means fewer variables, lower overheads for clients, and a more cohesive end product. </p>
                <p>The reason I can do this is that I'm no specialist. It is this very quality that enables me to: 1) forgo wearing blinkers, 2) see the big picture and 3) find unusual yet clever solutions to problems that my specialist colleagues—whom, it must be said, I have a great deal of respect for—might otherwise miss.</p>
            </details>
            <hr>
            <details>
                <summary>How do you think about design?</summary>
                <p>Design is an attitude. Good design is opinionated. Good design is choosing the right compromise for your audience. Good design is knowing when to bend the process in your favour. Skip steps when you deem them unnecessary. Backtrack when you're unsatisfied. Maybe start from the solution first, or operate on intuition, or make something just for the sake of making people smile.</p>
                <p>Good design understands the power of rythm and play, and knows how to use them. Good design is harder to spot than poor design. Good design may be impossible to achieve, but it's always worth trying.
            </details>
            <hr>
            <details>
                <summary>Why trust based marketing?</summary>
                <p>In an era when an increasing number of people distrust or ignore traditional marketing messages, the trust-based approach encourages people to choose you because they, well, trust you. Not because you chased them or pushed them to their limit. My work in this area incorporates everything I have learnt from working in inbound marketing, internal comms, content, exhibition design, and graphic design over the last 8 years.</p>
            </details>
            <hr>
            <details>
                <summary>What is your background?</summary>
                <p>When I’m not busy solving problems, you’ll find me supporting fellow artists by volunteering with Open Art Folke. Occasionally I write and make art myself with words and pixels.</p>
                <p>Before doing the work I now do, I graduated in Digital Arts from the University of Greenwich—which is where much of this all began—and I then spent six years between cultural institutions and the Film & TV industry.</p>
                <p>I now live in Folkestone, on the Kent coast, but I was born in Italy in a land called Ciociaria.</p>
            </details>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>On sustainable actions</h2>
            
            <p>It took around 4.6 billion years for a cosmic swirl of gas and dust to become a planet suitable for birds and flowers and children. The last thing I want to do is devote my time to furthering its destruction. As such, in 2025, I joined Creatives for Climate, a global network of creative professionals who are putting their skills to work in the fight for the climate. I'm also a member of Good Folk, a collective that offers pro-bono support to charities with a lot on their plate. </p>
            <p>Although my practice is far from perfect, I see every project as an opportunity to shape the change I wish to see. And for the record, I will not work with clients in the fossil fuel sector, or those directly associated with this industry.</p>
        </section>
